{"title":"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A jar has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> red candies and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> blue candies. Terry picks two candies at random, then Mary picks two of the remaining candies at random. Given that the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Probability\" title=\"Probability\">probability</a> that they get the same color combination, irrespective of order, is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/1/271cdd78c4c10cde94410584ce7282874413cb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m/n,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Relatively_prime\" title=\"Relatively prime\">relatively prime</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive_integer\" title=\"Positive integer\">positive integers</a>, find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/b/5db26f75f6218a382840f0ed34f5d36bcff61d33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"56\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The probability that Terry picks two red candies is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/7/09798295d2fef7416be2bacc73da86960c1a70ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{10 \\cdot 9}{20 \\cdot 19} = \\frac{9}{38}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"98\" height=\"38\" />, and the probability that Mary picks two red candies after Terry chooses two red candies is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/e/cae46a8a07fb05f92dcaae8fc8644aeb87509429.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{7\\cdot8}{18\\cdot17} = \\frac{28}{153}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"107\" height=\"38\" />.  So the probability that they both pick two red candies is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/2/152035ef59f8caec089aee7ed2121abc7d8ad64b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{38} \\cdot \\frac{28}{153} = \\frac{14}{323}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"120\" height=\"38\" />.  The same calculation works for the blue candies.  \n</p><p>The probability that Terry picks two different candies is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/2/f322fd3e53f4cd23bda14bab40a81bdbd11b8ca8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{20\\cdot10}{20\\cdot19} = \\frac{10}{19}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"98\" height=\"38\" />, and the probability that Mary picks two different candies after Terry picks two different candies is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc831a695cace0f63e5435c1dd6b07d0867b6ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{18\\cdot 9}{18\\cdot 17} = \\frac{9}{17}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"102\" height=\"38\" />. Thus, the probability that they both choose two different candies is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/b/44b11a98113611a2d8b8c7daaf92362d3e9f4088.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{10}{19}\\cdot\\frac{9}{17} = \\frac{90}{323}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"111\" height=\"38\" />.  Then the total probability is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/2/9e281ac310860beb6bc8499faab958586f45fcad.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2 \\cdot \\frac{14}{323} + \\frac{90}{323} = \\frac{118}{323}\\]\" width=\"160\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>and so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/f/a4f0221928207421c62c122909cde0d33d5a693c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$118 + 323 = \\boxed{441}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"141\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nIn the above calculations, we treated the choices as ordered; that is, Terry chose first one candy, then a second, and so on.  We could also solve the problem using unordered choices.  The probabilities calculated will all be the same, but the calculations will appear somewhat different.  For instance, the probability that Mary chooses two red candies after Terry chose two red candies will have the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/3/583d91e711acda913618af5f985a5836d05efd98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{{8\\choose 2}}{{18 \\choose 2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"32\" height=\"49\" />, and the probability that Terry chooses two different candies will have the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/2/78278c78187098a0022bdeb4f833036878e7edd1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{{10\\choose 1}\\cdot{10\\choose 1}}{{20\\choose2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"74\" height=\"49\" />.  It is not difficult to see that these yield the same results as our calculations above, as we would expect.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II\" title=\"2004 AIME II\">2004 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Answer_Key\" title=\"2004 AIME II Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2004\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 15\">15</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AIME Problems and Solutions\"> All AIME Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external t